 \begin{lemma}[Test consistency]
 \label{lem:test-consistency}
-Fix a partition $\mathcal P_i$ and a committed profile, and suppose no theory's distance to an on-path conditional equals $\eta$ exactly.
-Consider a record consisting of a finite prefix together with observations generated under that profile from some generation on.
-As generations accumulate, almost surely:
+Fix a partition $\mathcal P_i$ and a standing profile, and suppose every theory is either $\eta$-adequate or strictly inadequate at each visited pair (none sits exactly at the adequacy boundary).
+Consider a record consisting of a finite prefix together with observations generated under that profile from some period on.
+Because rejection is permanent and the theories are finite, the active set is monotone and eventually constant; and almost surely:
 \begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
-  \item at each pair recurrent under the profile, every $\eta$-adequate theory eventually satisfies the pair's constraint and every $\eta$-inadequate theory eventually violates it; and
-  \item at each pair frozen after the prefix (never revisited under the profile), the constraint is eventually constant, equal to the prefix verdict.
+  \item every theory that is $\eta$-inadequate at a recurrent pair, and every theory carried past $b_i$ by the frozen prefix alone, is eventually rejected; and
+  \item every remaining theory---$\eta$-adequate at every recurrent pair and below $b_i$ on frozen evidence---survives forever, except for a false rejection of probability at most $e^{-b_i}$.
 \end{enumerate}
-Consequently the active set is eventually constant, equal to the theories that are $\eta$-adequate at every recurrent pair and satisfy every frozen constraint.
+Consequently dissonance arises almost surely when every expressible theory falls under~(a); when some theory qualifies under~(b), dissonance is avoided with probability at least $1-e^{-b_i}$.
 \end{lemma}
 
 \begin{proof}
-A recurrent pair is visited in each episode with a fixed positive probability determined by the i.i.d.\ full-support state draw, so it accumulates observations without bound and, by the strong law of large numbers, $\hat p_i(\cdot\mid C,a)\to p_i^{*}(\cdot\mid C,a)$; the finite prefix contributes a vanishing fraction.
-The triangle inequality places $\TV(\hat p_i,\marg{i}m(C)(a))$ within any $\epsilon>0$ of $\TV(p_i^{*},\marg{i}m(C)(a))$ from some generation on; the genericity hypothesis converts strict inequalities into eventual acceptance and rejection, and the slack sequence absorbs finite-sample fluctuation.
-A frozen pair has a fixed count and empirical conditional after the prefix, so its constraint is constant.
-Intersecting finitely many pairwise constraints gives the eventual active set.
+A recurrent pair is visited each period with a fixed positive probability under the i.i.d.\ full-support draw, hence infinitely often; a frozen pair is visited only in the finite prefix and adds at most a fixed constant to $\log E_i^{m}$.
+If $m$ is inadequate at a recurrent pair, the evidence factor there has strictly positive expected log-growth (the information divergence), so by the strong law $\log E_i^{m}\to+\infty$ almost surely and $m$ is rejected; likewise if the frozen constant alone reaches $b_i$.
+If $m$ is $\eta$-adequate at every recurrent pair and its frozen evidence is below $b_i$, then $E_i^{m}$ is a nonnegative supermartingale, so Ville's inequality gives $\Pr(\sup_t\log E_i^{m}\ge b_i)\le e^{-b_i}$, and off that event $m$ is never rejected.
+Permanence of rejection and finiteness of $M(\mathcal P_i)$ make the active set monotone and eventually constant; collecting the two cases gives the dissonance dichotomy.
 \end{proof}
 
